architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "syon_08"
  title: Numeric error codes for parse errors, mirrored per language
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-08-23"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    SyonError (Rust), syon.Error (Go) and syon.SyonError (Python) carried
    only a Forbidden/Syntax kind plus a human-readable message. There was no
    stable, programmatic way to ask "is this specifically a duplicate-key
    error?" without matching on the message text, which is fragile across
    wording changes and impossible to keep in sync across three
    independently-written parsers (see ADR 0004).

    A three-digit numeric code was requested, banded by block. An earlier
    attempt at this banded the codes against a three-block language, with
    Block 2 for literal blocks and Block 3 for document fences, following
    the phase1 analyzer's numbering rather than the grammar spec's, and ADR
    0006 recorded the resulting divergence as an accepted cost.

    ADR 0007 then removed the literal escape hatch. That deleted one of the
    two contested block types, so the phase1 numbering and the grammar
    spec's numbering now agree, and a table banded against the three-block
    model would describe blocks the language no longer has.
  decision: |
    Attach a three-digit ErrorCode to every parse error in all three
    implementations, banded against the two-block model both the grammar
    spec and phase1 now share: 1-99 general, 101-199 Block 1 (records,
    including | block scalars), 201-299 Block 2 (document fences). Within a
    band the low two digits are reserved for the same kind of problem in a
    different block, so 112 and 212 would both mean "anchor used where
    forbidden."

    Decode-time failures, which happen after a successful parse and sit
    outside the block model entirely, take 11-19.

    The code is API; the message wording is not. Each implementation defines
    its own enum rather than sharing one, matching the per-language
    mirroring ADR 0004 established, and each pins its codes in a test so a
    renumbering cannot land silently. The full table, with per-implementation
    reachability, lives in spec/05-error-codes.md.

    Not every code is reachable in every implementation, and that is
    recorded rather than hidden. Rust carries a leading [ or { through as
    scalar text because a pre-parse scan cannot tell an indicator from
    ordinary content; Go rejects it. Go has no configurable indentation
    step. Codes defined but unreachable are marked reserved, so the three
    enums stay in lockstep and a number is never reused for a different
    meaning.
  outcome: |
    Implementing the codes surfaced a real bug, fixed in the same change: an
    unterminated ``` fence was silently accepted by the Rust parser, which
    absorbed the rest of the file as that document's body, while syon-go
    already rejected it. The two implementations disagreed on a document
    that ADR 0004's independent-implementation discipline says they must
    agree on. The Rust preflight scan now tracks fence-open/close state and
    reports SYON-202, matching Go.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - A caller can dispatch on a specific failure without matching message text, which is what made cross-implementation agreement checkable in the first place.
      - Writing the table forced an audit of every error site in all three implementations, which is how the unterminated-fence divergence was found.
      - The reserved markers make the gaps between implementations explicit and reviewable, rather than something a reader has to discover by testing.
    negative:
      - Three enums must be kept in step by hand. The Python binding's From impl is exhaustive so a missing Rust variant is a compile error, but nothing enforces the Go side beyond review and the pinned tests.
      - Codes are a compatibility surface. Once published, a number cannot be reused for a different meaning, so an error site that is later split into two needs a new code rather than a renumbering.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Keep the earlier three-block banding, with fences at 301-399
      reason: It bands against a language with a literal escape hatch, which ADR 0007 removed. Publishing numbers for blocks that no longer exist would preserve a divergence whose cause is gone.
    -
      name: A single shared code enum, generated for all three languages
      reason: Rejected as contrary to ADR 0004, which chose independent implementations over a shared core precisely so the two parsers can disagree visibly and be caught, rather than sharing a bug.
    -
      name: Structured error variants instead of numeric codes
      reason: A Rust enum with a variant per failure would be more idiomatic in Rust alone, but does not survive the FFI or the Go boundary, which is where the cross-implementation matching has to happen.
